  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Humanitarian System&#xa0;Transformation Through Local&#xa0;Humanitarian Leadership in DRC</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The challenges of achieving Local Humanitarian Leadership (LHL), despite its impact, arise in a context where increasing needs outpace increasing aid, and access is becoming increasingly restricted. Humanitarian actors&apos; actions are often in fragile and conflict -affected contexts, even though Local and National Humanitarian Actors (LNHAs) often have privileged access to enable sustainable service delivery to conflict -affected people. In this programme, the problem is defined as follows: Three major obstacles prevent further institutionalisation of LHL within the humanitarian system. First, international actors continue to define the priorities and mechanisms for humanitarian assistance within the system, particularly at the national level. Second, risks, including those associated with new locally led response models, are not sufficiently understood, and jointly addressed. Third, there is still a lack of (quality) funding for a diverse set of LNHAs.  To do this, the program will focus on strengthening the impact and ensuring that: People affected by a crisis benefit from a more egalitarian humanitarian system, better informed about risks, better funded and led at the local, territorial, provincial and national levels, the program aims to support civil society in strengthening its capacity to intervene and negotiate on existing capacities within communities (civil society) and to prepare in order to contribute effectively to preventing and responding to risks and humanitarian crises in their environment, and this through three major results.  Outcome 1: More equitable and locally -led humanitarian system.  The intervention first provides seed funding to local Non Governmental Organisations (NGOs) to engage in National Reference Group (NRG) development. Second, it supports LNHAs in determining and then implementing their NRG work programme, which may include joint advocacy, enabling equitable coordination, local government involvement (where possible) and peer learning.</narrative>
  </description>
